# Haryana Places, Monuments and Tourism

> Ask a Haryana examiner for an easy mark and the question will usually
> be "which place is famous for what?" Haryana packs the Mahabharata
> battlefield, a Harappan metropolis, Sultanate forts and three decisive
> Battles of Panipat into a small state. Place-to-fame pairs are asked
> in the HSSC CET and Haryana Police papers almost every year, so this
> lesson ties each place to one anchor fact you can recall in seconds.

## 1. Kurukshetra and Thanesar: the Land of the Gita

Kurukshetra is the best-known pilgrimage region of the state. Its
sacred sites sit close together and are asked as a group.

| Site | What it is |
| --- | --- |
| Brahma Sarovar | One of Asia's largest man-made ponds; huge bathing crowds on solar eclipses and Somavati Amavasya |
| Sannihit Sarovar | Believed to be where seven sacred Saraswatis meet; bathing on Amavasya or at an eclipse is held very meritorious |
| Jyotisar | Spot where Krishna is believed to have delivered the Bhagavad Gita to Arjuna, under a banyan tree |
| Sheikh Chehli's Tomb | Mughal-era tomb of a Sufi saint, protected by the ASI; Sheikh Chehli is linked by tradition with Dara Shikoh |
| Kurukshetra Panorama and Science Centre | Museum with a large painted panorama of the Mahabharata war |
| Thanesar | Old city now merged with Kurukshetra; capital of Harsha (c. 590-647 CE), visited by the Chinese pilgrim Xuanzang |

[definition]
**Gita Jayanti** marks the day the Gita was revealed (Shukla Ekadashi of
the month of Margashirsha). Kurukshetra holds the **International Gita
Mahotsav** around it every year.
[/definition]

Pehowa (also in Kurukshetra district) is an ancient Hindu tirtha on the
Saraswati, associated with rites for departed ancestors. Do not confuse
Pehowa with Pinjore: one is a pilgrimage town, the other a garden.

## 2. Panipat and Karnal: Battlefields of India

Panipat is called the **City of Weavers** (handloom and textile hub) and is
famous for three battles that changed Indian history.

| Battle | Year | Winner and loser |
| --- | --- | --- |
| First Panipat | 1526 | Babur defeated Ibrahim Lodi; Mughal rule began |
| Second Panipat | 1556 | Akbar's forces (under Bairam Khan) defeated Hemu |
| Third Panipat | 1761 | Ahmad Shah Abdali defeated the Marathas |

Panipat's monuments include **Kabuli Bagh** (a garden and mosque built by
Babur) and the **tomb of Ibrahim Lodi**, where the British put up a platform in 1866.
Kala Amb, associated with the Third Battle, is also in the region.

Karnal is named, by local tradition, after the Mahabharata figure **Karna**.
The **Battle of Karnal (1739)** saw Nader Shah of Persia defeat the Mughal
emperor Muhammad Shah. Karnal also has the National Dairy Research
Institute, the Karna Lake bird-watching spot, and is the hometown of
Kalpana Chawla, who studied there before going to space.

## 3. Hisar, Hansi, Agroha and Rakhigarhi: Ancient to Sultanate

| Place | Anchor fact |
| --- | --- |
| Hisar | Founded in 1354 by Firoz Shah Tughlaq as **Hisar-e-Firoza** ("fort of Firoz"); fort built 1354-56 with four gates |
| Firoz Shah Palace complex | Includes Lat ki Masjid and Gujari Mahal (built for his wife Gujri) |
| Hansi | Old fortified town with the **Asigarh Fort**; the Irish adventurer George Thomas ruled from here around 1798-1801 |
| Agroha | Mound of an ancient town, linked by tradition to King **Agrasen**; Agroha Dham is a temple complex dedicated to him |
| Rakhigarhi | Harappan site on the Ghaggar plain, in the Hisar region; ASI estimates up to 350 hectares |

[definition]
**Rakhigarhi** is the largest known site of the Indus-Harappan civilisation
by ASI estimate. Excavations began in 1963 and were repeated from 1997 to
2000, around 2011-16, and again from 2021. Finds include planned streets,
drains, a gold foundry, seals, weights and burials. A 2019 DNA study of
one skeleton found no steppe ancestry.
[/definition]

Hisar is also known as the "City of Steel" (galvanised iron and stainless
steel) and hosts Chaudhary Charan Singh Haryana Agricultural University
(1970). Remember Agroha with **Agrasen** and Hisar with **Firoz Shah**; the
two ancient-medieval pairs are the commonest swap trap.

## 4. South and West: Narnaul, Tosham, Sohna, Surajkund, Gurugram

| Place | District | Famous for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Narnaul | Mahendragarh | Chor Gumbad, Jal Mahal (Shah Quli Khan, 1588-89), Birbal ka Chhatta, Mirza Ali Jan stepwell |
| Tosham | Bhiwani | Tosham hill with sacred kunds and a Sanskrit rock inscription, assigned by scholars to about the 4th-5th century CE |
| Sohna | Gurugram | Sulphur hot springs |
| Surajkund | Faridabad | Reservoir attributed to the Tomara king Surajpal; **Surajkund International Crafts Mela**, begun in 1987, held about 1-15 February |
| Anangpur Dam | Faridabad | Built by Anangpal Tomar (8th century, per tradition) upstream of Surajkund |
| Gurugram | Gurugram | Sheetla Mata Mandir (a major temple and fair site); Pataudi Palace in the district |
| Sirsa | Sirsa | Sirsa Fort; the Ottu barrage on the Ghaggar |
| Kaithal | Kaithal | Name from "Kapisthal", the abode of Hanuman; Hanuman temple at his traditional birthplace |

Narnaul's Chor Gumbad is not a thieves' tomb in the criminal sense; it is a
double-storeyed tomb built by an Afghan noble, Jamal Khan, as his own
tomb in Firoz Shah Tughlaq's reign.

## 5. Panchkula, Pinjore and Morni: Hills and Gardens

**Pinjore Gardens** (now officially **Yadavindra Gardens**) is a Mughal-style
terraced garden of seven terraces in the Panchkula district, built under
Fidai Khan, foster-brother of Aurangzeb. It was later restored by the
Patiala ruler Maharaja Yadavindra Singh, after whom it was renamed. The
early medieval **Bhima Devi Temple complex** nearby is an open-air museum.

Morni Hills, about 35 km from Panchkula city, offer **Tikkar Taal** (twin
lakes with an HTC resort), the Thakur Dwar temple and an old Morni fort now
used as a nature-study centre. Morni is the
state's best-known hill getaway.

## 6. Tourism Machinery and Circuits

The **Haryana Tourism Corporation (HTC)** was set up in 1974, has its
headquarters in Chandigarh and runs tourist facilities across the state. Thematic groupings used in tourism brochures:

| Circuit | Typical places |
| --- | --- |
| Mahabharata / Pilgrimage | Kurukshetra, Jyotisar, Pehowa, Kaithal |
| Heritage and Fort | Hisar, Hansi, Tosham, Narnaul, Pinjore |
| Eco and Hills | Morni, Kalesar, Sultanpur |
| Harappan | Rakhigarhi, Farmana (Rohtak), Agroha |

[example]
A paper asks: "Which place is associated with the Gita being delivered?"
The tempting options are Kurukshetra (the district) and Jyotisar (the
exact spot). If both appear, choose Jyotisar; if only Kurukshetra
appears, choose it. For "Sheikh Chehli's tomb" the district is Kurukshetra,
not Hisar. Always read whether the question wants the town or the site.
[/example]

[summary]
Kurukshetra: Brahma Sarovar, Sannihit Sarovar, Jyotisar (Gita), Sheikh
Chehli's tomb, Panorama, Thanesar (Harsha). Panipat: three battles, 1526,
1556, 1761; City of Weavers. Karnal: 1739 battle, Karna Lake, Kalpana
Chawla. Hisar: Firoz Shah 1354; Agroha: Agrasen; Hansi: Asigarh Fort;
Rakhigarhi: largest Harappan site. Narnaul: Chor Gumbad, Jal Mahal.
Tosham: rock inscription. Surajkund: Crafts Mela since 1987 (Faridabad).
Pinjore: Yadavindra Gardens, Panchkula. Morni: Tikkar Taal. HTC: 1974.
[/summary]
